{"type":"message","id":"c47dfc4f","parentId":"7062f1a8","timestamp":"2026-06-03T00:20:27.773Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the full picture. There are 4 Algo orders but:\n\n1. DOT: Only TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET at $1.160 — NO STOP LOSS!\n2. ETH: Only TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET at $2084.35 — NO STOP LOSS!\n3. XRP: Only STOP_MARKET at $1.300 — NO TAKE PROFIT!\n4. LINK: Only TAKE_PROFIT_MARKET at $9.030 — NO STOP LOSS!\n\nSo DOT, ETH, LINK are missing Stop Loss, and XRP is missing Take Profit.\n\nFrom the earlier log analysis:\n- DOT/ETH/LINK SL: \"Order would immediately trigger\" → rejected → no SL\n- XRP TP: \"Order would immediately trigger\" → rejected → no TP\n- DOT/ETH/LINK TP: Successfully placed ✅\n- XRP SL: Successfully placed ✅\n\nBut wait - there's also the Quantity issue! All have `\"quantity\": \"0.0\"` (or \"0.000\", \"0.00\"). The `closePosition: true` flag means it's a close-position order, which should close the entire position regardless of quantity=0. That should be fine.\n\nSo the problem is:\n1. DOT, ETH, LINK → SL fails (\"would immediately trigger\"), monitor places TP only, SL gets cancelled\n2. XRP → TP fails (\"would immediately trigger\"), monitor places SL only, TP gets cancelled\n\nThe root cause is the same: stop prices too close to market → rejected by Binance.\n\nNow, with my ATR fix:\n- For NEW positions, the SL will be wider, reducing \"would immediately trigger\" rejections\n- But the existing positions still have no SL on DOT/ETH/LINK and no TP on XRP\n\nThe code flow:\n1. The monitor's main loop periodically calls `submit_initial_algo_orders()` for new positions\n2. `update_trailing_stop()` moves the SL and calls `sync_stop_loss_to_binance()` which re-submits SL and TP\n3. But `sync_stop_loss_to_binance()` calls `cancel_algo_orders()` FIRST, then re-submits both SL and TP\n\nSo the problem is: when `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` runs, it:\n1. Cancels ALL Algo orders for the symbol (kills both TP and SL)\n2. Re-submits SL\n3. Re-submits TP\n4. If SL submission fails → only TP is left (SL order never placed)\n5. If TP submission fails → only SL is left (TP order never placed)\n\nThe cycle creates the \"漏掉\" (missing) pattern: each cycle wipes all orders and re-submits, but failures mean one of the two is always missing.\n\nThis is actually a design issue in `sync_stop_loss_to_binance()` and `submit_initial_algo_orders()`. When re-submitting, it cancels ALL first, then re-submits. If ONE of the two fails, the other is lost.\n\nThe fix options:\n1. When SL submission fails, don't cancel the TP (and vice versa) — submit only the one that needs updating\n2. Or keep the current \"cancel all + re-submit all\" but add retry logic for the failed side\n\nThe simplest fix is option 1: in `sync_stop_loss_to_binance()`, instead of `cancel_algo_orders()` (which cancels ALL algo orders), only cancel the specific type that needs updating. But that's complex because we'd need to query existing orders first.\n\nAn even simpler fix: in `sync_stop_loss_to_binance`, don't cancel all — only cancel the specific side (SL or TP). But the function currently submits both.\n\nActually, let me re-read the function. In `sync_stop_loss_to_binance`:\n1. `cancel_algo_orders(sym_usdt)` - cancels ALL algo orders\n2. Submit new SL → if fails, SL is gone\n3. Submit new TP → if fails, TP is gone\n4. If SL fails but TP succeeds → only TP remains (SL lost)\n5. If TP fails but SL succeeds → only SL remains (TP lost)\n\nThe fix: don't cancel all then re-submit. Instead, only cancel the specific order type that changed. But that requires knowing which orders exist.\n\nSimpler fix: instead of cancel_all, cancel only the order type we're about to replace. We can query existing orders first or pass the desired change.\n\nOR, the simplest fix: after submitting both, if one fails, log a warning but don't leave the position unprotected. Re-submit with wider parameters.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The root issue is that the TP/SL levels are too close to market → rejected. My ATR fix addresses this for new positions. But for existing positions, the monitor needs to handle the case where one side fails.\n\nLet me propose to fix `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` to not use cancel-all. Instead, only cancel orders that would conflict:\n\n```python\ndef sync_stop_loss_to_binance(symbol, typ, new_sl, old_sl, tp_price=0):\n    # Cancel only STOP orders, not all orders\n    # Then submit new SL\n    # Then, if tp_price > 0, cancel only TP orders, then submit new TP\n```\n\nBut this is getting complex. Let me focus on what's actually happening and give a practical recommendation.\n\nThe actual problem flow:\n1. Every cycle, the monitor detects SL needs updating\n2. Calls `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` → cancels ALL → tries to submit SL → fails → tries to submit TP → succeeds\n3. Result: only TP is set, SL is gone\n\nThe fix in `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` should be: don't blindly cancel all. Instead, maintain separate cancel for each order type.\n\nLet me look at the `cancel_algo_orders` function to see if it can cancel specific types.\n\nActually, the real fix is simpler than I think. The `sync_stop_loss_to_binance` function is called to update SL. But it also re-submits TP (because cancel_all wiped it). The fix: keep the TP submission as a separate step, not bundled with SL update.\n\nBetter approach: don't cancel_all. Instead:\n1. Get existing Algo orders\n2. Only cancel the STOP orders (we're about to replace SL)\n3. Submit new SL\n4. If tp_price is provided, also cancel existing TP and re-submit\n\nBut actually, the cancel endpoint may not support filtering by type easily. Let me check.\n\nI think the easiest practical fix: instead of `cancel_algo_orders` (cancel all), parse existing orders and only cancel the specific type being changed. But that adds an API call.\n\nActually, let me look at the Binance API for cancelling algo orders. There are two endpoints:\n- DELETE /fapi/v1/algo/order?algoId=xxx — cancel specific\n- DELETE /fapi/v1/algo/openOrders?symbol=XXX — cancel all for a symbol\n\nThe second one can't filter by type. We'd need to use the first with specific IDs.\n\nLet me look at cancel_algo_orders in the code.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_fh9hegy2dKjHKsFqTonZ6557","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -n 'def cancel_algo_orders' ~/.openclaw/workspace/scripts/crypto_signal_monitor.py ~/.openclaw/workspace/scripts/binance_auto_trade.py 2>/dev/null","timeout":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":1355,"output":3246,"cacheRead":59904,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":64505,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1780445992351}}
