{"type":"message","id":"3376939f","parentId":"cbd358b0","timestamp":"2026-06-03T03:35:17.587Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All positions are now closed. The ETH position was the last one, and the approach worked (buy to pad then sell all).\n\nNow I need to fix the code issue with `round(pad_qty, 2)`. For ETH, precision is 3, and 0.00282 rounds to 0.003 with 3 decimal places. But the actual buy must be at least $20 notional, so `pad_qty = max(ceil(20/price), current_qty)` — no, the buy should be to reach $20 notional standalone.\n\nActually wait, the buy needs to be at least $20 notional on its own. So pad_qty should be at least `20 / price`. And it should be rounded up to the next step size.\n\nLet me fix the code. The pad buy amount should be:\n```python\nmin_buy_qty = 20.0 / current_price  # minimum for $20 notional\npad_qty = format_quantity(symbol, min_buy_qty, current_price)\n# Ensure it's at least one step (step size for ETH is 0.001)\n```\n\nBut `format_quantity` uses `round()` which can round down. For a safety margin, I should just use `min_buy_qty` and let Binance handle precision.\n\nActually, let me simplify the padding approach. Instead of computing `20/price - qty`, just compute `20/price` and round up:\n```python\npad_qty = 20.0 / current_price  # minimum standalone buy\npad_qty = max(pad_qty, 0)\n# Don't over-round — use format_quantity which respects symbol precision\npad_qty = format_quantity(symbol, pad_qty + 0.0001, current_price)  # small bump\n```\n\nWait, that adds 0.0001 which could round to the correct step. But for DOT (precision 1), 0.0001 would still round to 0.\n\nA simpler approach: just always buy the minimum qty needed for $20 notional, then sell everything. Don't bother computing pad from existing.\n\nLet me fix the code.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_ViNmZCHoeElWCpk9zf6m5248","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"~/.openclaw/workspace/scripts/